<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bari is a port city on the Adriatic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. The seat of an Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> from about 847 to 871 and later of the Byzantine catepan of Italy, it fell to the Normans in 1071. The relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> in 1087, made its basilica a major pilgrimage centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bari.jpg" alt="Bari" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bari_-_Lungomare_Imperatore_Augusto_%26_Mare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
